  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="FR">Social dialogue and labour relations law, processes and institutions</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="FR">Social dialogue is a vital component of decent work as well as a critical means to promote the transition to formality. Mechanisms for social dialogue are however significantly underdeveloped and underutilized in the informal economy.  In addition, addressing labour relations challenges through social dialogue is one of the starting points for creating conditions for decent work in the ASEAN. 

Over the past decade, digital labour platforms, encompassing both online web-based and location-based models, have experienced rapid growth. As of October 2025, focusing on global online web-based platforms and location-based platforms in sectors like taxi, delivery, healthcare, personal services, and tutoring, at least 653 active platforms were identified. Similarly, the platform economy has grown substantially in South Asia, and is within this context, there an increasingly important source of employment. There is a pressing need for a to develop a better an understanding of access to grievance-handling and labour dispute resolution mechanisms for platform workers in
South Asia. 

Therefore, building on knowledge gained in 2024-25, on grievance handling mechanisms available to platform workers in India the country scope of knowledge development will expand to include document grievance handling processes/mechanisms provided by major platforms across key sectors (transport, delivery, personal services) in Nepal and Sri Lanka . This study will also document the platform worker experiences navigating these grievance handling systems, understanding their perceived effectiveness, and identifying barriers from their perspective.</narrative>
  </description>
